From e1c09a207513fb0af0c8fa86b1052c8692bc0bea Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 21:03:59 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] NDS: the slice loop for the JIT inline, timers run when they
 overflow

What melonDS does once per scheduler slice (about 9,000 times a frame at
64 cycles) cost more than the JIT's code on a CPU-bound ROM. Every change
is exact: the same cycles, events and order at any slice.

- FastTimers (SetFastTimers; default off here): RunFrame runs a CPU's
  timers only once its time reaches TimerNext[cpu], the earliest overflow
  of its counting timers (at least every 2^30 cycles: RunTimers' cycles is
  an s32). RunTimer is additive, so the counters catch up then; a read of a
  counter goes through RunTimers first; TimerStart and RunTimers recompute
  TimerNext; the counters are caught up before a savestate is written and
  before sleep mode moves the times. Off: TimerNext stays 0 and the timers
  run every slice, as before.
- FastLoop (SetFastLoop; default off here; used with FastSched):
  RunSlicesJIT, RunFrame's loop for the JIT with the common cases inline:
  NextTarget; Execute<JIT>'s block loop for a running CPU (the block lookup
  without GetRXBase's call; region setup out of line; one compare for the
  region check, which differs from melonDS's only for a region whose end
  wraps past 2^32, the ARM9 BIOS's last mirror, where melonDS sets the same
  region up again for every block); a halted CPU that nothing interrupts;
  GPU3D::Run's idle case; no timer due on either CPU by the ARM9's time
  (TimerNextMin), which takes the ARM7's halted case without its timer
  check, and a busy ARM7's first pass without the checks just made;
  RunSystem's walk when an event is due. TotalScanlines is checked
  after an event only (only events change it); CPUStop is read once for the
  ARM7's DMA and the sleep check when nothing runs between. The scheduler's
  members, the ARMs and GPU3D go through addresses kept in registers
  (PinAddress, SCHED): the NDS object is large and GCC rebuilt each far
  member's address at every use.
- FastSched: RunSystem walks the set bits only (the same events, the same
  order, the same snapshot of the mask) and rebuilds SchedNext on the way
  (a debug build checks it against a full walk); CancelEvent of the event
  SchedNext holds sets it to 0, "to recompute", which RunSystem or
  NextTarget resolves (the divider and sqrt unit cancel and reschedule
  theirs on every parameter write); SchedNextUpdate walks the set bits
  only; ScheduleEvent reads CurCPU and the clock shift once and does
  Reschedule's work itself; SchedEvent is 64 bytes.
- ARMJIT keeps the code region's base (JitRXBase) for LookUpBlock instead
  of calling GetRXBase for every block.
